      "readings": [
        {
          "role": "first",
          "ref": "2 Kings 5:1-14",
          "refKey": "2Kgs.5.1-2Kgs.5.14",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "deliverance",
              "faith-and-trust",
              "grace"
            ],
            "image": [
              "water"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "wonder-and-awe"
            ],
            "function": [
              "healing-and-comfort"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 5,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "Go to, &c. — It was very natural for a king to suppose, that the king of Israel could do more than any of his subjects."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 5,
              "v_start": 10,
              "v_end": 10,
              "text": "Elisha sent — Which he did, partly, to exercise Naaman's faith and obedience: partly, for the honour of his religion, that it might appear he sought not his own glory and profit, but only God's honour, and the good of men."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 5,
              "v_start": 11,
              "v_end": 11,
              "text": "Was wroth — Supposing himself despised by the prophet."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 5,
              "v_start": 12,
              "v_end": 12,
              "text": "Are not, &c. — Is there not as great a virtue in them to this purpose? But he should have considered, that the cure was not to be wrought by the water, but by the power of God."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 5,
              "v_start": 13,
              "v_end": 13,
              "text": "My father — Or, our father. So they call him, to shew their reverence and affection to him."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "psalm",
          "ref": "Psalm 30",
          "refKey": "Ps.30",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "deliverance",
              "resurrection-and-life",
              "thanksgiving"
            ],
            "image": [
              "garment-and-robe"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "joy",
              "praise-and-exultation"
            ],
            "function": [
              "thanksgiving-offering"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 30,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "Cometh — Speedily and in due season."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 30,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "Mountain — My kingdom: kingdoms are usually called mountains in prophetical writings."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 30,
              "v_start": 9,
              "v_end": 9,
              "text": "Profit — What wilt thou gain by it? The dust - Shall they that are dead celebrate thy goodness in the land of the living? Or, shall my dust praise thee?"
            },
            {
              "chapter": 30,
              "v_start": 11,
              "v_end": 11,
              "text": "Sackcloth — Given me occasion to put off that sackcloth, which they used to wear in times of mourning, Esth iv, 1 chap. xxxv, 13 Isaiah xxxii, 11 Joel i, 13. Girded - With joy, as with a garment, surrounding me on every side."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 30,
              "v_start": 12,
              "v_end": 12,
              "text": "My glory — My tongue. David professes his confidence in God, intermixing prayer to him, ver. 1-8. Complains to God and prays for deliverance, ver. 9-18. Concludes with giving glory to God, and encouraging others to trust him, ver. 19-24. To the chief musician, A psalm of David."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "second",
          "ref": "Galatians 6:(1-6), 7-16",
          "refKey": "Gal.6.7-Gal.6.16",
          "tags": {},
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "God is not mocked — Although they attempt to mock him, who think to reap otherwise than they sow."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "For he that now soweth to the flesh — That follows the desires of corrupt nature. Shall hereafter of the flesh - Out of this very seed. Reap corruption - Death everlasting. But he that soweth to the Spirit - That follows his guidance in all his tempers and conversation. Shall of the Spirit - By the free grace and power of God, reap life everlasting."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 9,
              "v_end": 9,
              "text": "But let us not be weary in well doing — Let us persevere in sowing to the Spirit. For in due season - When the harvest is come, we shall reap, if we faint not."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 10,
              "v_end": 10,
              "text": "Therefore as we have opportunity — At whatever time or place, and in whatever manner we can. The opportunity in general is our lifetime; but there are also many particular opportunities. Satan is quickened in doing hurt, by the shortness of the time, Rev. xii, 12. By the same consideration let us be quickened in doing good. Let us do good - In every possible kind, and in every possible degree. Unto all men - neighbours or strangers, good or evil, friends or enemies. But especially to them who are of the household of faith. For all believers are but one family."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 11,
              "v_end": 11,
              "text": "Ye see how large a letter — St. Paul had not yet wrote a larger to any church. I have written with my own hand - He generally wrote by an amanuensis."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 12,
              "v_end": 12,
              "text": "As many as desire to make a fair appearance in the flesh — To preserve a fair character. These constrain you - Both by their example and importunity. To be circumcised - Not so much from a principle of conscience, as lest they should suffer persecution - From the unbelieving Jews. For the cross of Christ - For maintaining that faith in a crucified saviour is alone sufficient for justification."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 13,
              "v_end": 13,
              "text": "For neither they themselves keep the whole law — So far are they from a real zeal for it. But yet they desire to have you circumcised, that they may glory in your flesh - That they may boast of you as their proselytes, and make a merit of this with the other Jews."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 14,
              "v_end": 14,
              "text": "But God forbid that I should glory — Should boast of anything I have, am, or do; or rely on anything for my acceptance with God, but what Christ hath done and suffered for me. By means of which the world is crucified to me - All the things and persons in it are to me as nothing. And I unto the world - I am dead to all worldly pursuits, cares, desires, and enjoyments."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 15,
              "v_end": 15,
              "text": "For neither circumcision is anything, nor uncircumcision — Neither of these is of any account. But a new creation - Whereby all things in us become new."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 16,
              "v_end": 16,
              "text": "And as many as walk according to this rule -\n\n1. Glorying only in the cross of Christ.\n\n2. Being crucified to the world. And,\n\n3. Created anew. Peace and mercy be upon them, and upon the Israel, that is, the Church, of God - Which consists of all those, and those only, of every nation and kindred, who walk by this rule."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "gospel",
          "ref": "Luke 10:1-11, 16-20",
          "refKey": "Luke.10.1-Luke.10.11",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "call-and-discipleship",
              "kingdom-of-god",
              "mission-and-witness"
            ],
            "image": [
              "harvest",
              "lamb",
              "road-and-journey"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "resolve-and-sending"
            ],
            "function": [
              "sending-benediction"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 10,
              "v_start": 2,
              "v_end": 2,
              "text": "Pray ye the Lord of the harvest, that he would thrust forth labourers — For God alone can do this: he alone can qualify and commission men for this work. Matt. ix, 37."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 10,
              "v_start": 3,
              "v_end": 3,
              "text": "Matt. x, 16."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 10,
              "v_start": 4,
              "v_end": 4,
              "text": "Salute no man by the way — The salutations usual among the Jews took up much time. But these had so much work to do in so short a space, that they had not a moment to spare."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 10,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "A son of peace — That is, one worthy of it."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 10,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "Matt. x, 11."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 10,
              "v_start": 11,
              "v_end": 11,
              "text": "The kingdom of God is at hand — Though ye will not receive it."
            }
          ]
        }
